{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly too high in the first ~1–15 days (typically +0.3 to +1.5 €/t), but become too low beyond ~day 20 when strong rallies occur (often −2 to −6 €/t by days 25–30). Apply a piecewise bias: subtract ~0.5–1.0 early, add an increasing uplift late (ramping to +3–6 by day 30 in rally regimes).",
    "Trend underreaction / missing late acceleration: when the last 5–10 days of history show an upswing, the model projects a flatter path and misses convexity. Add a momentum/convexity term that increases with recent slope (e.g., if 10d change > +3, add +0.1 to +0.25 €/t per forecast day after day 15).",
    "Overshoot on short-term peaks: after a local spike in history, the model tends to keep levels elevated for ~1 week while realized prices mean-revert more quickly. If the last 3–5 days include a sharp jump (e.g., > +2 in 3 days), damp the next 3–7 forecast points by ~0.5–1.5.",
    "Troughs are handled better than peaks: downside mean reversion is captured relatively well, but upside breakouts are underpriced. Use asymmetric adjustment: stronger positive corrections in uptrends than negative corrections in downtrends.",
    "Mid-horizon dip too deep: around days ~12–20 the model often projects a larger pullback than realized (especially after mild declines). Reduce the magnitude of forecast drawdowns in this window by ~20–40% unless recent realized volatility is rising."
  ]
}
